Subject: [#94-3-321] PCR: Smug Nihilism

Eric Forst wrote 3/25/94: <If we find that two beliefs of which we are certain
lead to a logical contradiction, we reject the belief of which we are less
certain.>

This continuous, sloppy mixing of terminology is wearing thin.  You are certain
of knowledge, facts, truth.  You are uncertain of beliefs.  Beliefs are
relative.  Certainty is absolute, "free from doubt."  There is simply no such
thing as "less certain."  You are either certain or you are uncertain.  You can
be more uncertain or less uncertain.  Mere repetition of your position of "we
reject the belief of which we are less certain" over and over and over does not
increase it's intelligibility, it merely indicates fuzzy, wooden-headed,
relativistic argumentation.

You can hold beliefs that are logical contradictions of each other easily if you
have contradictory purposes underlying the beliefs.  You reject a belief if
pushed to an action which can achieve only one of the underlying purposes.

Eric again: <I make that statement groundlessly; I do not justify it in any way.
But the criticism that my statement is groundless or ultimately unprovable will
not impress me.>

PCR, if not grounded in subjective purpose, is simply smug nihilism.  Your
knowledge becomes entirely dependent on the strength of intellect of others who
criticize your positions.  You pick and choose who you want to be around and
listen to.  By default, you rule yourself by authority because you give yourself
over to others' ability to criticize.  This becomes your foundation of
knowledge, your ground.  You are literally blown around on the wind of whatever
others can convince you of with no anchor point internally.  This is classic
Greek skepticism and modern-day intellectual and moral nihilism.  Smugness is
inherent in all forms of authority worship such as relying on the criticism of
others to know anything.  Without acknowledgement of the true source of
knowledge, subjective purpose carried by social communication into consensual
reality, you have denied yourself any foundation for knowing right from wrong.
Subjective purpose is the root of knowing what is true and what is false, what
is good and what is bad, what is beautiful and what is ugly.

PCR relies on the abilities of others to determine right from wrong for you.
How is this different from the Skeptics advice to follow convention since you
can't be certain of anything?  It isn't any different.  PCR simply leads to
conventional authority worship with the ability to know right from wrong
entirely dependent on the qualities of those criticizing your beliefs and
judgments.  This is nihilism.  The smugness sets in because all you have to do,
is decide who is an authority (who has standing to criticize you) and who isn't.
If you really wanted to challenge yourself you would never stop seeking
criticism from the very strongest intellects around.  This is how Socrates lived
his life and it is why PCR simply seems like the Socratic method restated.  Most
people, in fact, stop seeking criticism from the strongest intellects and become
self-satisfied.

Eric again: <I would say that your subjective states of mind are objective
reality which other people are barred from knowing by technological limits, just
as technological limits used to bar people from the observation of erythrocytes,
distant galaxies, and electrons. But I would not elevate barriers of inadequate
technology to the level of an "epistemological boundary".

You should elevate them because they constitute an absolute unbroachable barrier
to knowing other people's thoughts.  Even as technology advances, allowing
closer and closer inspection of other people's thoughts, there will still be
Edelman's "qualia assumption" barring final broach of the epistemological
boundary between consensual reality and subjective reality.  This "qualia
assumption" cannot be broached without loss of self of the individual invaded.
The invaded person would become a tool or a program, would lose control of their
destiny, their self-determination, their uniqueness and the source of their
rights.  The fact that rational science (the Lockean movement in Western
civilization) is attempting to broach this final barrier to subjective reality
has prompted many people to become anti-rational, anti-technology, anti-science
(the Rousseauean movement in Western civilization) in order to preserve their
human free will.

Eric again: <I still contend that the distinction between subjective reality and
objective reality is wholly spurious; subjective reality is a subset of
objective reality. It's the part of objective reality that is taking place in
your nervous system.>

Objective reality is inaccessible to us due to epistemological boundaries.  We
approach it through science within consensual reality, the domain of knowledge
and meaning.  Subjective reality is completely accessible to us and us alone,
the domain of purpose and value.  These distinctions are very real, they are
aspects of the only existence there is, not subsets.  You will never know what I
am thinking under any circumstances short of the total loss of my self, but I
know what I am thinking.  When you examine my thoughts, you can be certain of
what you find within epistemological limits, I am objective reality to you.
When I examine my own thoughts, I am certain of them without epistemological
limits, I am subjective reality to myself.  The distinction is insurmountable
and crucial to any coherent epistemological method.

Eric again: <In MATTER AND CONSCIOUSNESS, Paul Churchland discusses the
fallibility of our access to our internal mental states. "Subjective" reality is
observed fallibly and imperfectly, just the same way that objective reality is
observed. Experimental evidence for this is well established, and Churchland
provides some good references to the literature. My observation of and reporting
on my present "subjective" mental state is neither more nor less fallible than
my observation of and reporting on the objects on my desk at this moment.>

Churchland's brand of functionalism and his arguments for the fallibility of
knowing your own thoughts are rejected by me in favor of the biological
epistemology of Edelman as modified by Kauffman's spontaneous order theory
applied to the neuronal somatic selection system and Searle's "no deep
unconscious" models.  It is silly to argue that I don't know what I am thinking
in subjective reality.  Knower-and-known systems such as this are whole, not
fragmented as Churchland posits.  Fallibility within consensual reality is
commonplace, that is, when you attempt to communicate your mental states and
thoughts to others.

Eric again: <Because utility is slippery and ill-defined, because there is no
standard of measurement for the "utile" that we can use to measure your utility
against mine, etc, utility is not a particularly useful criticism in and of
itself.>

Utility is whatever actions lead to achievement of subjective purpose.  This
isn't slippery or ill-defined at all.  It is subjective.  The measurement of
utility is memetic selectional fitness within consensual reality.

Eric again: <It is only useful for distinguishing between ideas which have
*already* been thoroughly analyzed for logical consistency, and have *already*
been experimentally tested against sense data, or that do not admit of
experimental test against sense data.>

Analysis is grounded in subjective purpose.  Logical consistency is grounded in
subjective purpose.  Experimental test methodology is grounded in subjective
purpose.  There is no criticism on the list of PCR-valid criticisms you have
previously posted that is not grounded in subjective purpose.  It's foundation
is subjective utility.

Eric again: <When a baby touches a candle and gets burned, it becomes reluctant
to touch candles. This is evidence of the presence of some memes about induction
and causality in the baby's mind.  Our "initial-state" memes are phenotypical
products of our genes.> and <Object permanence is not on the list of built-in
birth memes, but induction of causality from repeated correlation is.  Also, see
Chomsky on built-in grammatical structures, and the evidence about creole
languages which backs him up on this.>

Chomsky's built-in grammatical structures have been scientifically debunked with
certainty.  See Edelman on neuroembryology, topobiology and neuronal somatic
selection.  Memes are not "built-in" whatsoever, no "initial-states."  They form
within the neuronal somatic selection system through complex multicombinatorial
processes from "microhormonal" biophysical processes to neuronal attractors in
neuromorphological space.  Memes are propagated within consensual reality.

