Subject: [#94-3-324] PCR: Relative Boundary of Certainty

Steve Whitrow wrote 3/19/94: <I decided to start with the most basic certainties
-- "I think" and "I am", and these I see as statements about objective reality
as well as subjective reality.>

These are certainties in subjective reality, "I think, therefore I am."  Your
thoughts are private, no one has access to them as you experience them,
therefore they are solely subjective reality.  These two thoughts are an
expression of the purpose of survival, the homeostatic values instilled in us by
our genes, the default purpose at the most surface level of our subjective
reality.  They say something about objective reality, namely, the subjective "I"
has a boundary and beyond this boundary is non-self.  Non-self is objective
reality.  They also say something about consensual reality, namely, the language
expressing the concepts "I", "think", and "am" all have been imparted to the
subjective reality of the individual by social contact.  Without the social
contact, no such concepts (or the logic of thought) rise to this sophisticated
level of awareness.  So just being certain subjectively that "I think" and "I
am" automatically points to the existence of consensual reality and objective
reality.  All three aspects of reality are revealed by these certainties and
that is all there is to existence.  Therefore, "existence exists" is a
certainty.

The maybe-I-am-a-simulation possibility I have never seen as more than
sophistry, easily eliminatable with reflection over time on the richness of
sensory experience and the free will of thought to choose subjective purposes
and then act on those purposes.  Simulations do not have such powers of advanced
selectional systems in fitness space because the simulator retains control of
