Subject: [#94-3-325] Epistemological problem in environment discussion

    The First Extropian Squirrel has noted a disturbing pattern of
argument in the ongoing discussion of environmental problems.  It has to
do with the formal criteria used for asserting that a new phenomenon
exists; the point is that it may very well be reasonable to use
different criteria in, for example, preparing scientific papers for
submission to refereed journals, than in formulating social policy.  The
Squirrel expects that some of you will be familiar with the material he
is about to present, and apologizes for boring you.

    Let's develop a simple example to elaborate.  Suppose we are
studying the distribution of oak trees on a hillside.  Suppose that in
the square kilometer selected for study, there happen to be 1000 of
them.  Consider the question:  Are these trees randomly distributed or
not?  (We must be careful to specify just exactly what model of "random
distribution" we use, and in this case a likely one would be that the
number of oak trees per hectare is Poisson-distributed with mean 10, but
all that matters for the example is that we acknowledge that we are
comparing data to a random model of reality and seeing if the fit is
good.)
    In slightly more elaborate detail, we might be asked to decide which
of two hypotheses we think is true, either:
 
      1:  The oak trees are distributed randomly, or
      2:  Not!

The hypothesis of random behavior, of "no phenomenon present", is
traditionally called the "null hypothesis".  If we decide that the null
hypothesis is true, we can publish a short paper stating that we found
random distribution of oak trees in such-and-such a place, and can
explain how we did our statistics.  If we decide that hypothesis #2 is
true, we may optionally do some more work to find out what kind of
non-random pattern is present -- do the trees come in groves, do they
space themselves out uniformly, and so on.
    Note that each choice of which hypothesis to make is associated with
a particular kind of mistake.  These too have traditional names:

      A "type one error" consists of rejecting the null hypothesis
      falsely.  In this example, a type one error would be to assert
      that there is a systematic pattern to the oak tree distribution
      when in fact the distribution is random.

      A "type two error" consists of accepting the null hypothesis
      falsely.  In this example, a type two error would be to assert
      that the oak tree distribution was random when in fact a pattern
      was present.

    In deciding which hypothesis to claim is true, we must consider the
consequences of making a mistake.  We might well ask, "Suppose we make a
type one error.  So what?"  And ditto for a type two error.  Scientists
usually want to run a low risk of making a type one error.  Their peers
will laugh at them if they assert that a phenomenon is present when it
isn't.  They won't get tenure.  They will waste time and money trying to
elaborate the details of a non-existent pattern.  Thus if we end up
publishing a paper asserting that the oak tree distribution was
non-random, we would want to have done enough analysis to be able to say
something like "We are ninety-five percent confident that the oak tree
distribution is non-random."  Or choose some other confidence level if
you don't like 0.95.
    Of course, minimizing the probability of making a type one error
increases the probability of making a type two error.  The more
confident we are that we haven't made a false claim of a pattern being
present, the more susceptible our analysis is to ignoring a real pattern
by mistake.  We may miss out on a Nobel prize by ignoring the subtle
evidence of systematic oak tree distribution.  But that's the breaks.
Which would you rather have, credit or credibility?  Most scientists
prefer credibility, view a type one error as "worse" than a type two
one, and are thus very careful not to make rash claims that something is
going on.

    But for purposes other than doing science, a type two error can
sometimes be very grave.  For example, suppose your name is Macbeth, and
the hillside selected for our ecological study is Dunsinane, and you are
worried about the prophecy that your downfall shall happen when Birnham
Wood draws nigh.  In this case you might wish to be very trigger-happy
about non-random distributions of those trees, and take action of some
sort even on the slightest trace of clumping, even if it meant an
increased probability of making a type one error.

    A lot of the widely-discussed environmental disasters seem to many
people to be fairy tales, but their consequences if they do happen are
at least as grave as was the march of Birnham Wood for Macbeth.  Thus
even if the scientific results correctly conclude "the probability of
such-and-such a phenomenon being real is low", it is not necessarily
rational to ignore the prospect that the phenomenon exists.  What is
rational likely depends on things not evaluated in the pure scientific
work, such as "what *is* the probability of this phenomenon being real",
and "what is the magnitude and impact of the disaster that would follow
if it were".

                                Jay Freeman, First Extropian Squirrel

